# Docker matches a pattern that contains NO SLASH only at the context root. This file's whole
# spelling is shaped by that rule, because the obvious version of it leaks production secrets.
#
# A bare `.env` excludes ./.env and does NOT exclude supabase/selfhosted/.env -- and that second
# file is the Supabase stack's own .env, holding JWT_SECRET, the anon and service-role keys and the
# database password. The Dockerfile runs `COPY . .`, so a build whose context contains it bakes all
# of that into an image layer. Measured, not assumed: with root-only patterns the nested file was
# present in the built image.
#
# The live web deploy escaped this only because its .env happens to sit at its context root. The
# production stack's clone at /opt/britelink-production is an equally valid context for this same
# Dockerfile and does contain the nested .env, so the exposure was one `--build` away.
#
# Every path pattern below is `**/`-prefixed so it matches at any depth.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
**/.git

# Secrets. This repo generates real .env files in two separate places -- the web root, and
# supabase/selfhosted/ -- so a root-only rule covers one and leaks the other. The negated line
# re-includes the committed template, which carries placeholder values and is intentionally public.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

qa
ios
docs
coverage
**/*.md
!**/HOW-TO-RUN.md
